    "product_id": "9e2e0432-16bd-4c24-a3d4-6de168032e74",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 160716\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1216 AM PDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms expected today\n  and Thursday across our eastern zones.\n\n* Elsewhere, expect breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts through \n  the week with dry conditions and near-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nExpecting isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms this \nafternoon across Lincoln and Mohave counties, with chances\nspreading into Thursday morning. While the southern Great Basin \nremains situated under a dry southwesterly flow aloft, the \nsouthern Mojave Desert will experience a south-southeasterly flow,\nwhich will usher in 1.25 to 1.75 inches of PWAT into the region -\nspecifically southeastern Nevada and northwestern Arizona. In \naddition to the moisture, there is a disturbance riding up the \nBaja California that is currently highlighted by the NHC as having\na 10 percent chance of tropical storm formation in the next 48 \nhours. While this disturbance is unlikely to become a cyclone, as \nit pushes northward, the vorticity maxima associated with it will \nlead the development of showers and thunderstorms across southern \nMohave County / the lower Colorado River Valley this afternoon. \nAdditionally, energy riding along the upper-level trough across \nthe western CONUS has closed off over northern California this \nmorning and will begin its meander eastward across Nevada this \nafternoon. The enhanced jet associated with this closed low will \ncombine with daytime heating to facilitate convection across \neastern Lincoln, eastern Clark, and Mohave counties, which is in-\nline with the latest CAM guidance. That said, there is a noticable\nPWAT gradient across the center of our forecast area, which could\nact as a dryline, resulting in convection developing a bit \nfurther west than guidance currently suggests. Will continue to \nkeep an eye on locations along and west of Interstate 15 as the \nday progresses. As a result of ample instability and moisture, \nthunderstorms that form are expected to be efficient rain- makers \nwith frequent lightning, which will elevate the risk of flash \nflooding, particularly across Mohave County and underneath \ntraining storms. Forecast HRRR soundings in south-central Mohave \nCounty show over 1750 J/kg of SBCAPE, 750-1000 J/kg of DCAPE, 1.50\ninches of PWAT, and 500 mb cooling (LIs around -4 degrees C), so \nsevere thunderstorms are likely as well.\n\nThe trough axis associated with the aforementioned closed low will \npush through the forecast area today and Thursday, resulting in \nelevated south-southwesterly winds with gust speeds generally \nbetween 25 and 35 mph. Cannot rule out occasional gusts to 40 mph \nin the higher terrain. Otherwise, persistent troughing over the \nwestern CONUS will result in afternoon high temperatures near-\nnormal for mid-September. Cloud cover heading into overnight \nperiods may result in elevated morning lows, however.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nsoutherly winds tonight give way to another round of gusty south-\nsoutheast breezes Wednesday morning, gradually turning to a true \nsouth direction in the afternoon. On average, wind direction during \nfrom 16z-22z will be ~160, but brief periods as far east as 120 are \npossible. Gusts of 20-30 knots are likely (70%) from 17z - 04z. \nWinds weaken after sunset. Few to scattered clouds around 10kft with \ntemperatures remaining below 100F.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light winds tonight with \nanother round of gusty south-southeast breezes on Wednesday in most \nlocations. Gusts generally 20-30 knots, but would not be surprised \nto see a few ~35 knot readings in the Owens Valley and lower \nColorado River Valley. The latter will likely be induced by outflow \nfrom thunderstorms expected in western AZ. Best storm chances (40-\n60%) are in far southern and northern Mohave County Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. The more robust convection will be capable of \nproducing strong outflow winds, hail, and heavy rain. Few to \nscattered clouds around 10kft, with greater coverage in locations \nwith the best storm chances.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat \nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
